 # Changelog
 
+## Unreleased (wave 4, LZ lane: `claude/w4-lz-20261118`)
+
+Kolmo's own LZ coders against the libraries they would replace. Opt-in as before (`KOLMO_NATIVE=lz`, `lzma`): no
+default output changes, the determinism hashes are unchanged. Measurements in `benchmark/w4/lz/RESULTS.md`.
+
+### Changed
+- **KLZS (codec 31), a new byte-aligned block format for the LZ4 role**: literals, tokens, 16-bit offsets and length
+  extensions as four streams after a three-field header; a token of 4 + 4 bits. The decoder runs short sequences in
+  a budgeted loop with one test each. The fast parse hashes 6 bytes into a table of 16-bit positions. Against LZ4 -1
+  (4 MiB blocks, 19 files; the lane's measurement): −5.9% size, decode 1.05× (1 thread) / 1.06× (4 threads), compress
+  0.98× / 0.95×. `KOLMO_NATIVE=lz` writes it where rc3 wrote KLZ (codec 28). **Codec 28 keeps its rc3 format and
+  keeps decoding** (the lane had replaced the format of id 28 in place, so rc3's opt-in frames stopped decoding;
+  landed under its own id by review40/R01, `benchmark/review40/klzh2/RESULTS.md`).
+- **KLZH (codec 29) parse**: level 1 is a single-table parse with the repeat offset tried one byte ahead; every fast
+  level tries the second most recent offset right after each match. Same format: rc3 reads the new blocks and this
+  build reads rc3's, but the bytes written under the opt-in differ from rc3's. KLZH2 (codec 32) codes the same parse.
+- **KLZ, KLZH and khuf copy through `__builtin_memcpy`**: with `_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3` (the Ubuntu default) 16-byte
+  copies into buffers of run-time known size were calls to `__memcpy_chk` (+13% KLZH compress).
+
+### Measured and not kept
+- KLZMA decoder variants (branch-free normalisation, a padded input, select-based bits): all slower with GCC 13; the
+  same C built with clang decodes 13% faster. An assembly bit decoder is the next step (decode is 0.72-0.74× of
+  liblzma 5.8.1).
+
 ## Unreleased (rc3 wave 3, decmap lane: `claude/rc3w3-decmap-20261118`)
 
 Decode maps: prebuilt decoder tables stored in the frame were measured and rejected (Huffman tables cost 4-8 KiB per
diff --git a/benchmark/w4/lz/perfile.py b/benchmark/w4/lz/perfile.py
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..f3159f5b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/benchmark/w4/lz/perfile.py
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+"""Per-file component benchmark of the wave-4 LZ lane: each (codec, file, thread count) in its own natbench process,
+so peak RSS (from wait4) is the coder's plus the harness buffers (the same for both coders of a pair).
+
+usage: python3 benchmark/w4/lz/perfile.py build/natbench OUT.tsv BLOCK_KIB REPS THREADS PAIR [PAIR ...] -- FILE ...
+PAIR is rival:native (e.g. lz4:klz1). Prints and appends rows: file, threads, codec, in, out, c MB/s, d MB/s, RSS KiB.
+1 thread and N threads are separate invocations (benchmark/rc2/PROTOCOL.md)."""
+import os, subprocess, sys
+NB, OUT, BK, REPS, T = sys.argv[1:6]
+i = sys.argv.index("--"); pairs = [p.split(":") for p in sys.argv[6:i]]; files = sys.argv[i + 1:]
+def run(codec, f):
+    p = subprocess.Popen(["taskset", "-c", "0-3", NB, codec, T, BK, REPS, f], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
+    out = p.stdout.read(); _, st, ru = os.wait4(p.pid, 0)
+    if st: raise SystemExit(f"{codec} {f}: failed")
+    w = out.split()
+    return int(w[3]), int(w[5]), float(w[9]), float(w[12]), ru.ru_maxrss
+with open(OUT, "a") as o:
+    for f in files:
+        for a, b in pairs:
+            for c in (a, b):
+                n, z, cs, ds, rss = run(c, f)
+                row = f"{os.path.basename(f)}\tT{T}\t{c}\t{n}\t{z}\t{cs:.1f}\t{ds:.1f}\t{rss}"
+                print(row); o.write(row + "\n"); o.flush()
